              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source are both variables, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory regions. The use of the string copy function in this context is unsafe, as it does not handle overlapping memory correctly, which can lead to unpredictable program behavior or security issues. There is no evidence that the source or destination pointers are null at the call site, and the code does not appear to use the destination's capacity to determine the copy length. Additionally, there is no explicit guard to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. The presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((destination + (max_size - 1) <= source) || (source + (max_size - 1) <= destination)) {\n    std::strncpy(destination, source, max_size - 1);\n    destination[max_size - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before calling the string copy function. If overlap is detected, handle it safely according to your application's requirements.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as memmove. Replace the string copy with a call to memmove, and ensure null-termination:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t copy_len = max_size - 1;\nmemmove(destination, source, copy_len);\ndestination[copy_len] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach safely copies the data even if the source and destination overlap, and ensures the destination buffer is null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
